"""Triton implementation of aten::_foreach_addcmul_.Scalar (in-place).

self[i] = self[i] + value * tensor1[i] * tensor2[i]   (for every list entry i)

The reference returns None (void) and mutates the existing storage of self[i],
so no output tensors are allocated here; every entry is written in place.

Dispatch:
  * small total work  -> one fused launch over the whole (<=16 entry) list, so
    the per-call host cost is paid once instead of once per entry;
  * large total work  -> one lean launch per entry (its host cost hides under
    the device stream) with a full-tile, unmasked fast path.
Both paths write the same storage with ATen's sequential per-entry ordering.

Host cost control: on this backend a JIT launch spends a large, fixed amount of
Python time per argument (~4 us), which dominates the tiny 64x64 lists.  For the
common dense/equal-shape case the launch therefore goes through a directly
cached CompiledKernel: the Triton binder/key/compile-cache lookup runs once per
(distinct argument signature, i.e. dtype + int values + pointer alignment), and
later calls invoke the compiled launcher directly.  The kernel, its arguments
and the executed work are identical; only Python-side argument binding is
skipped.  Any failure of that path falls back to the ordinary
``kernel[grid](...)`` launch.
"""

